WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:06.896 --> 00:09.706 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference September 18,

00:09.706 --> 00:12.916 align:middle
2019 CHAIR POWELL.

00:12.916 --> 00:15.916 align:middle
Good afternoon, everyone,
and welcome.

00:15.946 --> 00:18.556 align:middle
My colleagues at the Federal
Reserve and I are dedicated

00:18.556 --> 00:19.856 align:middle
to serving the American people.

00:20.996 --> 00:24.186 align:middle
We do this by steadfastly
pursuing the goals Congress has

00:24.186 --> 00:26.786 align:middle
given us: maximum
employment and stable prices.

00:27.176 --> 00:30.656 align:middle
We are committed to making the
best decisions we can based

00:30.656 --> 00:32.496 align:middle
on facts and objective analysis.

00:33.586 --> 00:35.496 align:middle
Today we decided to
lower interest rates.

00:36.126 --> 00:38.226 align:middle
As I will explain
shortly, we took this step

00:38.226 --> 00:41.076 align:middle
to help keep the U.S.
economy strong in the face

00:41.076 --> 00:43.766 align:middle
of some notable developments
and to provide insurance

00:43.826 --> 00:45.316 align:middle
against ongoing risks.

00:45.856 --> 00:49.496 align:middle
The U.S. economy has
continued to perform well.

00:50.066 --> 00:52.696 align:middle
We are into the 11th year
of this economic expansion,

00:53.286 --> 00:55.276 align:middle
and the baseline outlook
remains favorable.

00:56.196 --> 00:58.946 align:middle
The economy grew at
a 2½ percent pace

00:58.996 --> 01:00.546 align:middle
in the first half of the year.

01:01.776 --> 01:04.486 align:middle
Household spending-supported
by a strong job market,

01:04.696 --> 01:08.266 align:middle
rising incomes, and solid
consumer confidence-has been the

01:08.266 --> 01:09.436 align:middle
key driver of growth.

01:09.906 --> 01:13.546 align:middle
In contrast, business investment

01:13.546 --> 01:16.716 align:middle
and exports have weakened amid
falling manufacturing output.

01:17.726 --> 01:20.236 align:middle
The main reasons appear
to be slower growth abroad

01:20.476 --> 01:22.866 align:middle
and trade policy
developments-two sources

01:22.866 --> 01:25.016 align:middle
of uncertainty that we've
been monitoring all year.

01:25.496 --> 01:28.046 align:middle
Since the middle of last year,

01:28.046 --> 01:30.736 align:middle
the global growth
outlook has weakened,

01:31.136 --> 01:32.756 align:middle
notably in Europe and China.

01:33.766 --> 01:35.966 align:middle
Additionally, a number
of geopolitical risks,

01:35.966 --> 01:37.936 align:middle
including Brexit,
remain unresolved.

01:38.936 --> 01:41.466 align:middle
Trade policy tensions
have waxed and waned,

01:41.706 --> 01:43.536 align:middle
and elevated uncertainty
is weighing

01:43.536 --> 01:45.266 align:middle
on U.S. investment and exports.

01:45.706 --> 01:48.506 align:middle
Our business contacts around
the country have been telling us

01:48.536 --> 01:51.546 align:middle
that uncertainty about trade
policy has discouraged them

01:51.546 --> 01:52.916 align:middle
from investing in
their businesses.

01:53.896 --> 01:56.416 align:middle
Business fixed investment
posted a modest decline

01:56.416 --> 01:58.716 align:middle
in the second quarter, and
recent indicators point

01:58.716 --> 01:59.926 align:middle
to continued softness.

02:00.976 --> 02:04.156 align:middle
Even so, with household spending
remaining on a solid footing

02:04.156 --> 02:05.826 align:middle
and with supportive
financial conditions,

02:06.376 --> 02:08.076 align:middle
we expect the economy
to continue

02:08.116 --> 02:09.646 align:middle
to expand at a moderate rate.

02:10.816 --> 02:14.256 align:middle
As seen from FOMC participants'
most recent projections,

02:14.386 --> 02:18.206 align:middle
the median expectation for
real GDP growth remains near 2

02:18.206 --> 02:20.976 align:middle
percent this year and
next before edging

02:20.976 --> 02:23.246 align:middle
down toward its estimated
longer-run value.

02:24.646 --> 02:26.016 align:middle
The job market remains strong.

02:26.276 --> 02:29.276 align:middle
The unemployment rate has
been near half-century lows

02:29.276 --> 02:32.426 align:middle
for a year and a half, and
job gains have remained solid

02:32.526 --> 02:33.346 align:middle
in recent months.

02:33.836 --> 02:36.966 align:middle
The pace of job gains
has eased this year,

02:37.416 --> 02:41.446 align:middle
but we had expected some slowing
after last year's strong pace.

02:42.656 --> 02:44.756 align:middle
Participation in the
labor force by people

02:44.756 --> 02:47.106 align:middle
in their prime working
years has been increasing.

02:48.356 --> 02:50.106 align:middle
And wages have been
rising, particularly

02:50.106 --> 02:51.336 align:middle
for lower-paying jobs.

02:51.566 --> 02:54.216 align:middle
People who live and work in low-

02:54.216 --> 02:56.486 align:middle
and middle-income
communities tell us that many

02:56.486 --> 02:59.756 align:middle
who have struggled to find work
are now getting opportunities

02:59.756 --> 03:02.276 align:middle
to add new and better
chapters to their lives.

03:03.426 --> 03:05.456 align:middle
This underscores, for
us, the importance

03:05.456 --> 03:06.846 align:middle
of sustaining the expansion

03:07.386 --> 03:09.486 align:middle
so that the strong job
market reaches more

03:09.486 --> 03:10.476 align:middle
of those left behind.

03:11.916 --> 03:13.736 align:middle
We expect the job
market to remain strong.

03:14.846 --> 03:16.526 align:middle
The median of participants'
projections

03:16.526 --> 03:19.056 align:middle
for the unemployment rate
remains below 4 percent

03:19.136 --> 03:20.346 align:middle
over the next several years.

03:22.396 --> 03:24.996 align:middle
Inflation continues to
run below our symmetric 2

03:24.996 --> 03:25.836 align:middle
percent objective.

03:26.366 --> 03:27.996 align:middle
Over the 12 months through July,

03:28.696 --> 03:32.916 align:middle
total PCE inflation was 1.4
percent and core inflation,

03:33.556 --> 03:38.306 align:middle
which excludes volatile food and
energy prices, was 1.6 percent.

03:39.166 --> 03:41.746 align:middle
We still expect inflation
to rise to 2 percent;

03:41.926 --> 03:44.516 align:middle
the median projection
is 1.9 percent this year

03:44.606 --> 03:46.456 align:middle
and 2 percent in 2021.

03:47.056 --> 03:50.616 align:middle
However, inflation pressures
clearly remain muted,

03:50.616 --> 03:53.476 align:middle
and indicators of longer-term
inflation expectations are

03:53.476 --> 03:55.496 align:middle
at the lower end of
their historical ranges.

03:55.806 --> 03:59.836 align:middle
We are mindful that continued
below-target inflation could

03:59.836 --> 04:02.336 align:middle
lead to an unwelcome
downward slide

04:02.336 --> 04:04.256 align:middle
in longer-term inflation
expectations.

04:05.656 --> 04:08.256 align:middle
Overall, as we say in our
postmeeting statement,

04:08.256 --> 04:11.536 align:middle
we continue to see sustained
expansion of economic activity,

04:11.906 --> 04:13.546 align:middle
strong labor market conditions,

04:13.896 --> 04:16.366 align:middle
and inflation near our
symmetric 2 percent objective

04:16.496 --> 04:17.346 align:middle
as most likely.

04:18.686 --> 04:20.746 align:middle
While this has been our
outlook for quite some time,

04:21.356 --> 04:23.106 align:middle
our views about the
path of interest rates

04:23.106 --> 04:25.346 align:middle
that will best achieve
these outcomes have changed

04:25.396 --> 04:26.906 align:middle
significantly over
the past year.

04:27.856 --> 04:29.596 align:middle
As I mentioned, weakness
in global growth

04:29.596 --> 04:32.706 align:middle
and trade policy uncertainty
have weighed on the economy

04:32.706 --> 04:34.336 align:middle
and pose ongoing risks.

04:35.046 --> 04:38.606 align:middle
These factors, in conjunction
with muted inflation pressures,

04:39.196 --> 04:41.146 align:middle
have led us to shift our views

04:41.146 --> 04:42.776 align:middle
about appropriate
monetary policy

04:42.776 --> 04:46.266 align:middle
over time toward a lower path
for the federal funds rate,

04:46.796 --> 04:48.726 align:middle
and this shift has
supported the outlook.

04:49.516 --> 04:50.916 align:middle
Of course, this is the role

04:50.916 --> 04:53.516 align:middle
of monetary policy-to
adjust interest rates

04:53.606 --> 04:55.196 align:middle
to maintain a strong
labor market

04:55.616 --> 04:57.776 align:middle
and keep inflation near
our 2 percent objective.

04:59.906 --> 05:02.146 align:middle
Today's decision to lower
the federal funds rate target

05:02.146 --> 05:06.986 align:middle
by ¼ percent, to 1.75 percent
to 2 percent, is appropriate

05:06.986 --> 05:09.496 align:middle
in light of the global
developments I mentioned as well

05:09.496 --> 05:11.036 align:middle
as muted inflation pressures.

05:11.976 --> 05:14.646 align:middle
Since our last meeting, we
have seen additional signs

05:14.646 --> 05:16.286 align:middle
of weakness abroad
and a resurgence

05:16.286 --> 05:18.636 align:middle
of trade policy tensions,
including the imposition

05:18.636 --> 05:19.686 align:middle
of additional tariffs.

05:20.846 --> 05:23.956 align:middle
The Fed has no role in the
formulation of trade policy,

05:23.956 --> 05:26.196 align:middle
but we do take into
account anything

05:26.196 --> 05:28.716 align:middle
that could materially
affect the economy relative

05:28.716 --> 05:30.386 align:middle
to our employment
and inflation goals.

05:30.916 --> 05:34.826 align:middle
The future course of
monetary policy will depend

05:34.826 --> 05:38.086 align:middle
on how the economy evolves
and what developments imply

05:38.086 --> 05:40.436 align:middle
for the economic outlook
and risks to the outlook.

05:40.936 --> 05:43.856 align:middle
We have often said that policy
is not on a preset course,

05:44.296 --> 05:45.706 align:middle
and that is certainly
the case today.

05:45.706 --> 05:48.916 align:middle
As I have noted, the
baseline economic outlook

05:48.916 --> 05:49.786 align:middle
remains positive.

05:50.656 --> 05:52.576 align:middle
The projections of
appropriate policy show

05:52.576 --> 05:56.246 align:middle
that participants generally
anticipate only modest changes

05:56.386 --> 05:59.116 align:middle
in the federal funds rate
over the next couple of years.

05:59.626 --> 06:03.146 align:middle
Of course, those views are
merely forecasts and, as always,

06:03.556 --> 06:06.216 align:middle
will evolve with the
arrival of new information.

06:06.696 --> 06:12.016 align:middle
Let me say a few words about
our monetary policy operations.

06:13.176 --> 06:15.936 align:middle
Funding pressures in money
markets were elevated this week,

06:15.936 --> 06:18.636 align:middle
and the effective federal
funds rate rose above the top

06:18.636 --> 06:20.066 align:middle
of its target range yesterday.

06:21.156 --> 06:23.656 align:middle
While these issues are
important for market functioning

06:23.656 --> 06:26.386 align:middle
and market participants,
they have no implications

06:26.386 --> 06:28.826 align:middle
for the economy or the
stance of monetary policy.

06:30.236 --> 06:32.626 align:middle
This upward pressure
emerged as funds flowed

06:32.626 --> 06:34.306 align:middle
from the private
sector to the Treasury

06:34.486 --> 06:37.006 align:middle
to meet corporate tax
payments and settle purchases

06:37.006 --> 06:38.146 align:middle
of Treasury securities.

06:39.196 --> 06:40.196 align:middle
To counter these pressures,

06:40.196 --> 06:43.386 align:middle
we conducted overnight
repurchase operations yesterday

06:43.386 --> 06:44.126 align:middle
and again today.

06:45.356 --> 06:47.206 align:middle
These temporary operations
were effective

06:47.206 --> 06:48.616 align:middle
in relieving funding pressures,

06:48.936 --> 06:51.056 align:middle
and we expect the federal
funds rate to move back

06:51.186 --> 06:52.256 align:middle
into the target range.

06:53.226 --> 06:54.906 align:middle
In addition, as we
have done in the past,

06:55.066 --> 06:57.396 align:middle
we made a technical adjustment
to the interest rate paid

06:57.396 --> 06:59.386 align:middle
on required and excess
reserve balances,

06:59.996 --> 07:03.426 align:middle
setting it 20 basis points below
the top of the target range

07:03.426 --> 07:04.456 align:middle
for the federal funds rate.

07:05.216 --> 07:07.516 align:middle
In a related action, we
also adjusted the rate

07:07.516 --> 07:09.976 align:middle
on the overnight
repurchase facility

07:09.976 --> 07:12.656 align:middle
to 5 basis points below the
bottom of the target range.

07:12.656 --> 07:15.676 align:middle
We will continue to
monitor market developments

07:15.676 --> 07:19.306 align:middle
and will conduct operations
as necessary to foster trading

07:19.306 --> 07:22.166 align:middle
in the federal funds market at
rates within the target range.

07:23.206 --> 07:25.866 align:middle
Consistent with our decision
earlier this year to continue

07:25.866 --> 07:28.806 align:middle
to implement monetary policy
in an ample-reserves regime,

07:29.386 --> 07:33.016 align:middle
we will, over time, provide a
sufficient supply of reserves

07:33.396 --> 07:35.666 align:middle
so that frequent
operations are not required.

07:36.266 --> 07:40.266 align:middle
To summarize, we are fully
committed to pursuing our goals

07:40.266 --> 07:42.396 align:middle
of maximum employment
and stable prices.

07:43.036 --> 07:45.446 align:middle
As the Committee
contemplates the future path

07:45.446 --> 07:47.176 align:middle
of the target range for
the federal funds rate,

07:47.716 --> 07:49.606 align:middle
it will continue to
monitor the implications

07:49.606 --> 07:53.186 align:middle
of incoming information for the
economic outlook and will act

07:53.186 --> 07:55.026 align:middle
as appropriate to
sustain the expansion,

07:55.646 --> 07:56.736 align:middle
with a strong labor market

07:56.736 --> 07:59.716 align:middle
and inflation near its
symmetric 2 percent objective.

08:00.036 --> 08:02.076 align:middle
Thanks. I'll be happy
to take your questions.

08:03.466 --> 08:05.886 align:middle
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

08:06.026 --> 08:08.436 align:middle
Marty Crutsinger with
the Associated Press.

08:08.606 --> 08:12.656 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, when you cut rates
in July, you characterized it

08:12.656 --> 08:14.676 align:middle
as a "midcycle adjustment."

08:14.676 --> 08:17.186 align:middle
Is that still your view
of what's happening?

08:17.826 --> 08:18.986 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:19.116 --> 08:22.516 align:middle
So, as you can see from
our policy statement

08:22.516 --> 08:25.296 align:middle
and from the SEP, we see a
favorable economic outlook,

08:25.846 --> 08:28.166 align:middle
with continued moderate
growth, a strong labor market,

08:28.166 --> 08:30.016 align:middle
and inflation near our
2 percent objective.

08:30.136 --> 08:33.546 align:middle
And, by the way, that view
is consistent with those

08:33.546 --> 08:34.826 align:middle
of many other forecasters.

08:36.146 --> 08:39.276 align:middle
As you can see, FOMC
participants generally think

08:39.276 --> 08:41.926 align:middle
that these positive economic
outcomes will be achieved

08:41.926 --> 08:44.866 align:middle
with modest adjustments
to the federal funds rate.

08:45.446 --> 08:49.466 align:middle
At the last press conference,
I pointed to two episodes in,

08:49.466 --> 08:53.266 align:middle
I guess, 1995 and 1998 as
examples of such an approach,

08:53.266 --> 08:57.356 align:middle
which was successful in
both of those instances.

08:58.206 --> 09:01.196 align:middle
As our statement also
highlights, though,

09:01.196 --> 09:03.776 align:middle
there are risks to this positive
outlook due particularly

09:03.776 --> 09:05.826 align:middle
to weak global growth
and trade developments.

09:05.826 --> 09:07.556 align:middle
And if the economy
does turn down,

09:08.356 --> 09:09.876 align:middle
then a more extensive sequence

09:09.876 --> 09:11.666 align:middle
of rate cuts could
be appropriate.

09:12.316 --> 09:15.886 align:middle
We don't see that-it's not what
we expect-but we would certainly

09:15.886 --> 09:17.546 align:middle
follow that path if
it became appropriate.

09:18.286 --> 09:21.826 align:middle
In other words, as we say in
our statement, we will continue

09:21.826 --> 09:24.126 align:middle
to monitor these developments
closely, and we will act

09:24.126 --> 09:25.406 align:middle
as appropriate to help ensure

09:25.406 --> 09:26.816 align:middle
that the expansion
remains on track.

09:27.216 --> 09:29.516 align:middle
RICHARD MILLER.

09:31.726 --> 09:32.786 align:middle
[Inaudible remark] I'm sorry.

09:33.836 --> 09:35.266 align:middle
Rich Miller with Bloomberg.

09:35.266 --> 09:40.046 align:middle
Taking the statement, your
opening remarks, and the SEP,

09:40.226 --> 09:41.716 align:middle
I'm just wondering what kind

09:41.716 --> 09:43.106 align:middle
of message we should
take from this.

09:43.106 --> 09:46.366 align:middle
Do you still-does
the FOMC-is it safe

09:46.366 --> 09:50.006 align:middle
to say the FOMC still has
an easing bias or not?

09:50.086 --> 09:50.656 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:51.736 --> 09:55.046 align:middle
So the idea of having
a bias is something

09:55.046 --> 09:57.496 align:middle
that was a longtime practice,
and we don't actually have

09:57.496 --> 09:58.496 align:middle
that practice anymore,

09:58.496 --> 10:00.466 align:middle
so I can't really
adopt it right here.

10:00.466 --> 10:03.436 align:middle
But, nonetheless, I'll
respond to your question.

10:03.606 --> 10:07.366 align:middle
So we did-we made one decision
today, and that decision was

10:07.396 --> 10:09.716 align:middle
to lower the federal funds
rate by ¼ percentage point.

10:10.116 --> 10:11.516 align:middle
We believe that action
is appropriate

10:12.226 --> 10:14.596 align:middle
to promote our objectives,
of course.

10:15.156 --> 10:18.076 align:middle
We're going to be highly
data dependent, as always.

10:18.076 --> 10:20.146 align:middle
Our decisions are going to
depend on the implications

10:20.226 --> 10:24.256 align:middle
of incoming information for the
outlook, and I would also say,

10:24.256 --> 10:26.346 align:middle
as we often do, that we're
not on a preset course.

10:27.186 --> 10:30.606 align:middle
So that's how we're
going to look at it.

10:30.606 --> 10:33.376 align:middle
We're going to be carefully
looking at economic data.

10:33.446 --> 10:38.256 align:middle
Sometimes the path ahead is
clear and sometimes less so.

10:38.256 --> 10:40.796 align:middle
So we're going to be looking
carefully, meeting by meeting,

10:41.456 --> 10:44.116 align:middle
at the full range of
information, and we're going

10:44.116 --> 10:47.256 align:middle
to assess the appropriate
stance of policy as we go.

10:47.256 --> 10:50.316 align:middle
And, as I said, we
will act as appropriate

10:50.316 --> 10:51.306 align:middle
to sustain the expansion.

10:51.306 --> 10:51.706 align:middle
RICHARD MILLER.

10:51.706 --> 10:53.626 align:middle
If I could just follow
up, you've also said

10:53.626 --> 10:55.846 align:middle
that the favorable
outlook is predicated

10:55.846 --> 11:00.526 align:middle
on financial conditions, and
the financial conditions are

11:00.526 --> 11:02.236 align:middle
in turn predicated on an outlook

11:02.236 --> 11:05.476 align:middle
for Fed policy-in this
case, further cuts.

11:06.006 --> 11:11.476 align:middle
Wouldn't that suggest that you
should be inclined to-if you

11:11.476 --> 11:13.906 align:middle
want those financial conditions
and that favorable outlook

11:13.906 --> 11:15.616 align:middle
to come about, you
should be inclined to cut?

11:16.866 --> 11:17.156 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

11:17.696 --> 11:21.586 align:middle
Well, what we do going forward
is very much going to depend,

11:21.586 --> 11:24.636 align:middle
Rich, on the flow of
data and information.

11:24.636 --> 11:27.776 align:middle
We've seen-you know, if you look
at the things we're monitoring,

11:27.776 --> 11:30.906 align:middle
particularly global growth
and trade developments,

11:31.586 --> 11:33.276 align:middle
global growth has
continued to weaken.

11:33.566 --> 11:36.566 align:middle
I think it's weakened
since our last meeting.

11:37.166 --> 11:39.846 align:middle
Trade developments have been
up and down and then up,

11:39.846 --> 11:42.216 align:middle
I guess-or back up,
perhaps-over the course

11:42.216 --> 11:43.046 align:middle
of this intermeeting period.

11:43.046 --> 11:44.666 align:middle
In any case, they've
been quite volatile.

11:44.666 --> 11:47.856 align:middle
So we do see those risks as
actually more heightened now.

11:48.506 --> 11:49.736 align:middle
We're going to be
watching that carefully.

11:49.736 --> 11:52.636 align:middle
We're also going to be watching
the U.S. data quite carefully,

11:53.096 --> 11:55.146 align:middle
and we'll have to make
an assessment as we go.

11:55.706 --> 11:59.736 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

12:03.756 --> 12:04.716 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

12:04.716 --> 12:06.566 align:middle
Nick Timiraos of the
Wall Street Journal.

12:07.286 --> 12:09.856 align:middle
I know you're trying to
speak for the Committee

12:09.976 --> 12:11.696 align:middle
when you do these
press conferences,

12:11.696 --> 12:14.406 align:middle
but the Committee is
clearly divided right now,

12:14.406 --> 12:17.776 align:middle
at least about the outlook and
the appropriate policy path.

12:17.776 --> 12:20.896 align:middle
Some people think you need to
wait and see the labor market

12:20.896 --> 12:24.226 align:middle
and the consumer crack or weaken
further before acting more

12:24.226 --> 12:27.156 align:middle
aggressively, and some
people think that by the time

12:27.156 --> 12:29.466 align:middle
that happens, you'll need

12:29.466 --> 12:32.426 align:middle
to do even more aggressive
action to arrest a downturn.

12:33.216 --> 12:34.916 align:middle
Where do you stand on this?

12:35.686 --> 12:37.276 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:37.626 --> 12:41.426 align:middle
Well, let me just say,
on the general point

12:41.546 --> 12:43.746 align:middle
of diverse perspectives,
you're right.

12:43.746 --> 12:46.446 align:middle
Sometimes the-and there have
been many of those times

12:46.446 --> 12:48.156 align:middle
in my now almost eight
years at the Fed,

12:48.636 --> 12:51.046 align:middle
many times when the
direction is relatively clear

12:51.046 --> 12:53.166 align:middle
and it's relatively
easy to reach unanimity.

12:53.166 --> 12:56.246 align:middle
This is a time of difficult
judgments and, as you can see,

12:56.346 --> 12:57.786 align:middle
disparate perspectives.

12:57.786 --> 13:01.796 align:middle
And I really do think
that's nothing but healthy,

13:02.016 --> 13:07.106 align:middle
and so I see a benefit in having
those diverse perspectives,

13:07.256 --> 13:07.586 align:middle
really.

13:08.066 --> 13:09.456 align:middle
So your question, though, is?

13:09.456 --> 13:09.786 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

13:09.786 --> 13:11.586 align:middle
This is to your own view,
because the data is,

13:11.586 --> 13:12.746 align:middle
to some extent, lagged,

13:13.086 --> 13:15.566 align:middle
especially when you have
these risks on the horizon,

13:16.006 --> 13:17.946 align:middle
and the markets obviously think

13:17.996 --> 13:20.376 align:middle
that these risks could
materialize more than what you

13:20.696 --> 13:22.426 align:middle
and your colleagues
are projecting

13:22.426 --> 13:23.406 align:middle
in the dot plot today.

13:23.406 --> 13:27.606 align:middle
So, I wonder, where are your
own views about the tension

13:27.606 --> 13:30.346 align:middle
between risk management,
which implies some degree

13:30.346 --> 13:33.346 align:middle
of data independence, and this
idea of being data dependent?

13:33.996 --> 13:34.686 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

13:34.686 --> 13:37.166 align:middle
Yes. So I'll try to
get at that this way.

13:37.646 --> 13:41.436 align:middle
I think that the idea that if
you see trouble approaching

13:41.436 --> 13:42.966 align:middle
on the horizon, you
steer away from it

13:42.966 --> 13:45.536 align:middle
if you can-I think that's
a good idea in principle,

13:45.536 --> 13:49.006 align:middle
and I think history teaches us
that it's better to be proactive

13:49.006 --> 13:51.496 align:middle
in adjusting policy if you can.

13:52.056 --> 13:56.996 align:middle
I think applying that principle
in a particular situation is

13:57.306 --> 13:58.686 align:middle
where the challenge comes.

13:59.266 --> 14:03.296 align:middle
So I told you where
the Committee-the bulk

14:03.296 --> 14:05.966 align:middle
of the Committee is
going, meeting by meeting,

14:06.006 --> 14:09.646 align:middle
and I think the main takeaway
is that this is a Committee

14:09.646 --> 14:13.356 align:middle
that has shifted its policy
stance repeatedly, consistently,

14:13.476 --> 14:16.966 align:middle
through the course of the year
to support economic activity

14:16.966 --> 14:18.676 align:middle
as it has felt that
it's appropriate.

14:18.736 --> 14:21.696 align:middle
At the beginning of the
year we were looking

14:21.696 --> 14:23.726 align:middle
at further rate increases,
then we were patient,

14:24.226 --> 14:26.066 align:middle
and then we cut once,
then we cut again.

14:26.756 --> 14:30.696 align:middle
And I think you've seen us being
willing to move based on data,

14:30.696 --> 14:32.996 align:middle
based on the evolving risk
picture, and I have no reason

14:32.996 --> 14:34.056 align:middle
to think that will change.

14:34.106 --> 14:37.126 align:middle
I think-but it will continue
to be data dependent,

14:37.126 --> 14:40.096 align:middle
and depend-data includes
the evolving risk picture.

14:40.596 --> 14:41.486 align:middle
That's where I am, and that's

14:41.486 --> 14:43.566 align:middle
where I think the bulk
of the Committee is.

14:44.556 --> 14:45.756 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

14:46.226 --> 14:48.826 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, I wonder
if you're-oh, sorry.

14:49.226 --> 14:50.586 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

14:50.666 --> 14:53.186 align:middle
I've only done this a
dozen-a lot of times.

14:53.186 --> 14:54.856 align:middle
I wonder if you're concerned

14:54.856 --> 14:57.206 align:middle
about how the Federal
Reserve operated

14:57.386 --> 15:00.266 align:middle
through the recent
liquidity crunch in markets.

15:00.266 --> 15:02.246 align:middle
We talked to many traders

15:02.246 --> 15:06.006 align:middle
who said the tax date payment
was known well in advance.

15:06.006 --> 15:10.656 align:middle
There were several reports of
people pointing to September

15:10.986 --> 15:13.286 align:middle
as a potential crunch time.

15:13.316 --> 15:16.586 align:middle
You closed Monday at the top
end of the fed funds rate.

15:17.046 --> 15:19.906 align:middle
Tuesday came along, and
there wasn't an operation

15:19.906 --> 15:21.386 align:middle
until nine o'clock

15:21.386 --> 15:23.806 align:middle
and no announcement
until-of a second operation

15:23.806 --> 15:25.346 align:middle
until four o'clock
in the afternoon.

15:25.726 --> 15:29.506 align:middle
Was the Fed listening to
markets well ahead of time,

15:29.506 --> 15:32.426 align:middle
going back a year when
there were blowouts

15:32.426 --> 15:35.066 align:middle
in the overnight
rate at year-end

15:35.066 --> 15:36.166 align:middle
and the turn of the year?

15:36.526 --> 15:39.166 align:middle
Are you concerned
about how, for example,

15:39.166 --> 15:40.446 align:middle
the New York Fed
operated through this?

15:40.446 --> 15:41.076 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:41.176 --> 15:45.656 align:middle
You know, so I would say I
doubt that anyone is closer to

15:45.656 --> 15:46.916 align:middle
and has more invested

15:46.916 --> 15:49.266 align:middle
in carefully following
the-you know,

15:49.266 --> 15:50.606 align:middle
the behavior of these markets.

15:50.606 --> 15:53.926 align:middle
So, of course, we were
well aware of the-you know,

15:53.926 --> 15:56.236 align:middle
the tax payments and
also of the settlement

15:56.236 --> 15:57.606 align:middle
of the large bond purchases.

15:58.296 --> 16:01.986 align:middle
And, you know, we were
very much waiting for that.

16:01.986 --> 16:04.276 align:middle
But we didn't expect-the
response

16:04.276 --> 16:05.616 align:middle
to that was stronger
than we expected.

16:05.616 --> 16:06.566 align:middle
And, by the way, our sense is

16:06.566 --> 16:09.486 align:middle
that it surprised market
participants a lot, too.

16:09.486 --> 16:12.006 align:middle
You know, people were
writing about this

16:12.006 --> 16:13.606 align:middle
and publishing stories
about it weeks ago.

16:13.606 --> 16:14.436 align:middle
It wasn't a surprise,

16:14.436 --> 16:18.006 align:middle
but it was-it was a stronger
response than-certainly

16:18.006 --> 16:19.716 align:middle
than we expected.

16:20.136 --> 16:24.706 align:middle
So, no, I'm not concerned about
that, to answer your question.

16:25.446 --> 16:27.766 align:middle
I can go on a little bit about
how we're looking at that.

16:27.766 --> 16:28.996 align:middle
Why don't I do that?

16:28.996 --> 16:33.286 align:middle
So, as I mentioned, it
doesn't-we don't see this

16:33.286 --> 16:36.106 align:middle
as having any implications
for the broader economy

16:36.106 --> 16:37.186 align:middle
or for the economic outlook,

16:37.356 --> 16:39.186 align:middle
nor for our ability
to control rates.

16:39.856 --> 16:42.596 align:middle
The strains in the money
markets reflect forces

16:42.596 --> 16:44.796 align:middle
that we saw coming, and they
just had a bigger effect

16:44.796 --> 16:48.136 align:middle
than I think most folks
anticipated-strong demand

16:48.136 --> 16:50.346 align:middle
for cash to purchase Treasuries
and pay corporate taxes.

16:50.346 --> 16:53.866 align:middle
We took appropriate actions
to address those pressures,

16:53.866 --> 16:55.926 align:middle
to keep the fed funds rate
within the target range,

16:55.926 --> 16:57.506 align:middle
and those measures
were successful.

16:57.506 --> 17:00.306 align:middle
If we experience another episode
of pressures in money markets,

17:00.606 --> 17:02.526 align:middle
we have the tools to
address those pressures.

17:02.976 --> 17:04.896 align:middle
We will not hesitate
to use them.

17:05.506 --> 17:06.816 align:middle
And since we're talking
about this,

17:06.816 --> 17:08.516 align:middle
let me take a step
back and say this.

17:09.176 --> 17:12.986 align:middle
Earlier in the year, as you will
all recall, after careful study

17:12.986 --> 17:14.576 align:middle
over a period of
years, actually,

17:14.576 --> 17:15.786 align:middle
the Committee announced
the decision

17:15.786 --> 17:18.686 align:middle
to implement monetary policy
in an ample-reserves regime.

17:19.756 --> 17:23.496 align:middle
And we've been operating in
that regime for a full decade.

17:23.496 --> 17:26.716 align:middle
We think it works well to
implement our rate decisions.

17:27.266 --> 17:30.036 align:middle
The main hallmark of that regime
is that we use adjustments

17:30.036 --> 17:31.986 align:middle
in our administered
rates-the IOER

17:31.986 --> 17:35.346 align:middle
and RRP rates-to keep the fed
fund rates in the target range.

17:35.606 --> 17:38.066 align:middle
It's designed specifically
so that we do not expect

17:38.066 --> 17:40.906 align:middle
to be conducting frequent
open market operations

17:41.416 --> 17:42.206 align:middle
for that purpose.

17:42.856 --> 17:44.256 align:middle
So, going forward, we're going

17:44.256 --> 17:46.586 align:middle
to be very closely
monitoring market developments

17:46.586 --> 17:49.086 align:middle
and assessing their implications
for the appropriate level

17:49.086 --> 17:53.016 align:middle
of reserves, and we're going
to be assessing, you know,

17:53.016 --> 17:54.926 align:middle
the question of when
it will be appropriate

17:54.926 --> 17:57.736 align:middle
to resume the organic
growth of our balance sheet.

17:58.166 --> 18:00.036 align:middle
And I'm sure we'll be revisiting

18:00.036 --> 18:02.146 align:middle
that question during
this intermeeting period

18:02.146 --> 18:03.826 align:middle
and certainly at
our next meeting.

18:03.826 --> 18:04.156 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

18:04.416 --> 18:05.336 align:middle
Could I just follow
up here quickly?

18:05.336 --> 18:07.256 align:middle
Do you think you've
underestimated the amount

18:07.256 --> 18:09.356 align:middle
of reserves necessary
for the banking system?

18:09.526 --> 18:09.926 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:10.076 --> 18:11.436 align:middle
So we've always said

18:11.876 --> 18:15.196 align:middle
that it's-that the level
is uncertain, right?

18:15.196 --> 18:18.086 align:middle
And that's something we've
tried to be very clear about.

18:18.736 --> 18:23.176 align:middle
And as you know, we've invested
lots of time talking to many

18:23.176 --> 18:24.596 align:middle
of the large holders of reserves

18:24.596 --> 18:28.206 align:middle
to assess their-what they say
is their demand for reserves.

18:28.206 --> 18:30.726 align:middle
We try to assess what that is.

18:30.726 --> 18:31.956 align:middle
We've tried to combine
all of that together.

18:31.956 --> 18:34.026 align:middle
We put it out so the
public can react to it.

18:34.616 --> 18:36.456 align:middle
But, yes, there's
real uncertainty,

18:36.456 --> 18:38.676 align:middle
and it is certainly
possible that we will need

18:38.676 --> 18:40.046 align:middle
to resume the organic growth

18:40.046 --> 18:42.236 align:middle
of the balance sheet
earlier than we thought.

18:42.446 --> 18:44.166 align:middle
That's always been
a possibility,

18:44.166 --> 18:45.106 align:middle
and it certainly is now.

18:45.156 --> 18:47.966 align:middle
Again, we'll be looking at
this carefully in coming days

18:47.966 --> 18:49.796 align:middle
and taking it up at
the next meeting.

18:51.056 --> 18:52.606 align:middle
BRENDAN GREELEY.

18:53.616 --> 18:57.316 align:middle
Brendan Greeley with
the Financial Times.

18:57.616 --> 19:00.216 align:middle
Earlier this year-or,
actually, in September,

19:00.216 --> 19:03.056 align:middle
the Governors' Board put out a
research paper looking-trying

19:03.056 --> 19:04.666 align:middle
to quantify trade uncertainty.

19:05.226 --> 19:07.346 align:middle
And it suggested
that it could drag

19:07.346 --> 19:10.546 align:middle
through the business investment
channel on growth as much

19:10.546 --> 19:12.426 align:middle
as a percentage point over
the course of the next year.

19:12.816 --> 19:15.906 align:middle
How much confidence do you
have in the Fed's ability

19:15.906 --> 19:19.306 align:middle
to estimate the real
effects of trade uncertainty?

19:19.566 --> 19:21.926 align:middle
And if you have confidence
in that paper, it would seem

19:21.926 --> 19:24.816 align:middle
to suggest a-sort of a more
aggressively dovish path

19:24.816 --> 19:25.696 align:middle
than the one that you've chosen.

19:26.206 --> 19:26.646 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:26.646 --> 19:29.326 align:middle
Yes. So I think to
provide a little context,

19:29.376 --> 19:32.076 align:middle
Fed economists do
research all the time.

19:32.756 --> 19:34.356 align:middle
It's generally of
very high quality.

19:34.596 --> 19:35.696 align:middle
It's their research.

19:35.696 --> 19:38.756 align:middle
It's not an official finding
of the Federal Reserve Board

19:38.756 --> 19:39.856 align:middle
or the Federal Reserve System.

19:40.436 --> 19:44.346 align:middle
It's just-and, by the way, they
put it out for public review

19:44.346 --> 19:47.416 align:middle
so that you can see their
econometrics, so you can see all

19:48.026 --> 19:50.746 align:middle
of it-their whole work
is exposed to critique

19:50.816 --> 19:51.666 align:middle
by the whole profession.

19:51.666 --> 19:53.816 align:middle
So it's a great tradition
that we have.

19:54.536 --> 19:57.886 align:middle
And, you know, what this
particular piece of work did is,

19:58.226 --> 20:01.236 align:middle
it went after measuring
trade policy uncertainty

20:01.236 --> 20:04.466 align:middle
through a couple of channels,
including concerning tariffs,

20:04.806 --> 20:06.556 align:middle
also concerning the
threat of more tariffs,

20:06.706 --> 20:09.346 align:middle
and it looked deeply
at the data to try

20:09.346 --> 20:10.926 align:middle
to assess the effects on output.

20:11.506 --> 20:13.986 align:middle
And while I would say, directly
answering your question,

20:14.016 --> 20:16.726 align:middle
there's real uncertainty around
these-around these effects.

20:16.726 --> 20:19.386 align:middle
It's a $22 trillion economy.

20:19.386 --> 20:21.336 align:middle
To try to isolate the effects

20:21.336 --> 20:22.906 align:middle
of certain things
is very challenging,

20:23.596 --> 20:25.076 align:middle
but we do the best we can.

20:25.556 --> 20:29.016 align:middle
So-and this piece of research
found significant effects,

20:29.016 --> 20:32.546 align:middle
and that's frankly
consistent with a number

20:32.546 --> 20:36.156 align:middle
of other research projects that
economists have undertaken.

20:36.156 --> 20:37.926 align:middle
It's also consistent with
what we've been hearing

20:37.926 --> 20:38.636 align:middle
in the Beige Book.

20:39.366 --> 20:41.936 align:middle
So, I mean, I think if you
take a step back from that,

20:41.936 --> 20:44.746 align:middle
we do feel that trade
uncertainty is having an effect.

20:44.746 --> 20:49.996 align:middle
You see it in weak business
investment, weak exports-hard

20:50.176 --> 20:51.786 align:middle
to quantify it precisely,
though.

20:54.496 --> 20:55.956 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

20:57.046 --> 20:58.876 align:middle
Hi, Howard Schneider
with Reuters.

20:59.176 --> 21:01.046 align:middle
I was struck by the sort

21:01.046 --> 21:07.296 align:middle
of anchored median federal
funds rate here through 2020,

21:07.296 --> 21:09.656 align:middle
and that in comparison to the
fact that you now have sort

21:09.656 --> 21:12.146 align:middle
of three very discrete
groups of opinions

21:12.576 --> 21:15.376 align:middle
around where the fed
funds rate is heading.

21:15.376 --> 21:16.906 align:middle
And I wondered, is
it fair to say

21:16.906 --> 21:21.306 align:middle
that the-those opinions
have become sort of firmer

21:21.306 --> 21:25.556 align:middle
in their conviction, and that
it's going to take some sort

21:25.556 --> 21:28.296 align:middle
of real material change in
the outlook now for that

21:28.296 --> 21:29.706 align:middle
to move in either direction?

21:29.706 --> 21:30.036 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

21:30.346 --> 21:32.176 align:middle
And you're asking
specifically about 2020?

21:32.176 --> 21:32.446 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

21:32.446 --> 21:34.716 align:middle
Well, the fact that this-the
fed funds rate is now seen

21:34.716 --> 21:37.166 align:middle
as not moving through
2020 suggests to me

21:37.166 --> 21:39.076 align:middle
that these opinions are
pretty-pretty well anchored

21:39.076 --> 21:40.766 align:middle
right now in those groups.

21:41.436 --> 21:41.826 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

21:41.826 --> 21:44.176 align:middle
You know, honestly,
I think it's hard

21:44.176 --> 21:47.836 align:middle
to have hardened expectations
about where rate policy is going

21:47.836 --> 21:48.656 align:middle
to be a year from now.

21:49.286 --> 21:53.156 align:middle
I think the closer you
get to the current day,

21:53.156 --> 21:54.376 align:middle
the more confidence
you can have.

21:54.376 --> 21:55.816 align:middle
But even then, knowing

21:55.816 --> 21:58.046 align:middle
where the-knowing
what the data will say

21:58.176 --> 22:02.976 align:middle
and the way geopolitical events
and other events are going

22:02.976 --> 22:05.226 align:middle
to evolve in the next 90 days
and the implications of that

22:05.226 --> 22:07.006 align:middle
for the economy, I
would say there's a lot

22:07.006 --> 22:08.186 align:middle
of uncertainty around that.

22:08.186 --> 22:08.646 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

22:09.246 --> 22:10.546 align:middle
Fair enough, but
if- CHAIR POWELL.

22:10.546 --> 22:14.436 align:middle
Particularly, if you look
at 2020, I think the use

22:14.436 --> 22:16.366 align:middle
of this is, individual
participants write

22:16.366 --> 22:17.376 align:middle
down their forecasts.

22:17.376 --> 22:19.186 align:middle
It should give you a sense
of how people are thinking

22:19.186 --> 22:24.126 align:middle
about the likely path of the
economy and the appropriate path

22:24.366 --> 22:27.836 align:middle
for monetary policy in that
individual person's thinking,

22:27.996 --> 22:29.646 align:middle
and I think that's a
good thing to know.

22:29.646 --> 22:33.336 align:middle
I think I'd be very reluctant
to look at it as hardened views

22:33.336 --> 22:34.836 align:middle
or a prediction, really.

22:35.246 --> 22:35.756 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

22:35.756 --> 22:36.846 align:middle
But just to follow
up, if I could.

22:36.846 --> 22:38.876 align:middle
There were a number
of arguments in July

22:38.876 --> 22:40.556 align:middle
around the reason
for cutting rates.

22:40.616 --> 22:43.166 align:middle
Have any of those gotten
substantially weaker

22:43.166 --> 22:44.736 align:middle
or changed around the table?

22:45.636 --> 22:45.946 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:46.006 --> 22:48.186 align:middle
You know, well, I think, if
you look at the U.S.-look

22:48.186 --> 22:52.716 align:middle
at the U.S. economy, the U.S.
economy has generally performed

22:52.716 --> 22:58.096 align:middle
roughly as expected-roughly:
The consumer is spending

22:58.096 --> 23:02.056 align:middle
at a healthy clip, I'd say
business fixed investment

23:02.056 --> 23:03.596 align:middle
and exports have
weakened further,

23:03.596 --> 23:06.136 align:middle
and I'd say the manufacturing
PMI suggests more

23:06.136 --> 23:06.716 align:middle
weakness ahead.

23:06.716 --> 23:08.156 align:middle
The labor market is
still strong, though,

23:08.156 --> 23:10.786 align:middle
so generally that is the same.

23:10.786 --> 23:14.826 align:middle
I think if you look at-the
global economy, I think,

23:14.826 --> 23:17.626 align:middle
has weakened further in the
EU and China, and I think,

23:17.626 --> 23:20.326 align:middle
you know, trade policy
developments have been a big

23:20.326 --> 23:24.046 align:middle
mover of markets and
of sentiment during

23:24.046 --> 23:24.886 align:middle
that intermeeting period.

23:24.886 --> 23:26.946 align:middle
So that's why I think
what's-that's what's happened

23:26.946 --> 23:31.066 align:middle
over the intermeeting period,
and, you know, different people

23:31.066 --> 23:33.536 align:middle
around the table have
different perspectives

23:33.536 --> 23:35.166 align:middle
as you obviously know.

23:35.626 --> 23:38.076 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

23:38.636 --> 23:40.536 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

23:42.966 --> 23:45.356 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek with
the New York Times.

23:45.646 --> 23:48.626 align:middle
I'm just curious on your
balance sheet point.

23:48.626 --> 23:50.366 align:middle
You talked about the
Committee thinking

23:50.366 --> 23:53.796 align:middle
about resuming organic
growth, you know, over time.

23:54.196 --> 23:55.356 align:middle
I guess the question is,

23:55.596 --> 23:58.826 align:middle
if you have shrunk your balance
sheet maybe just a little bit

23:58.876 --> 24:02.636 align:middle
too small to the point that
reserves are too scarce to get

24:02.636 --> 24:05.586 align:middle
through sort of these unusual
periods, is organic growth

24:05.586 --> 24:08.156 align:middle
in the balance sheet enough
to get back to a point

24:08.156 --> 24:10.766 align:middle
of ample reserves that can get
us through those tough times,

24:11.136 --> 24:11.776 align:middle
or would you need

24:11.866 --> 24:13.246 align:middle
to see something a
little bit above that?

24:13.246 --> 24:15.176 align:middle
And is that a possibility
the Committee would consider?

24:15.176 --> 24:16.356 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:16.356 --> 24:19.616 align:middle
You know, I think
it's hard to deal

24:19.616 --> 24:22.106 align:middle
with every hypothetical
possibility.

24:22.106 --> 24:24.126 align:middle
I think, for the
foreseeable future,

24:24.796 --> 24:28.136 align:middle
we're going to be looking at,
if needed, doing the sorts

24:28.136 --> 24:30.516 align:middle
of things that we did the last
two days-these temporary open

24:30.516 --> 24:31.296 align:middle
market operations.

24:31.296 --> 24:32.756 align:middle
That will be the
tool that we use.

24:33.216 --> 24:35.266 align:middle
And the question will
be, then-as we go

24:35.266 --> 24:41.036 align:middle
through quarter-end, as
we learn more, you know,

24:41.506 --> 24:43.586 align:middle
what really-how much of
this really has to do

24:43.586 --> 24:45.406 align:middle
with the level of reserves?

24:45.926 --> 24:48.036 align:middle
And I think we'll learn quite
a lot in the next six weeks.

24:49.646 --> 24:50.616 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

24:54.616 --> 24:56.176 align:middle
Victoria Guida with Politico.

24:56.536 --> 24:58.476 align:middle
Another money market question.

24:58.956 --> 25:02.286 align:middle
You know, banks have been
pointing to liquidity rules

25:02.286 --> 25:04.806 align:middle
as having contributed to some
of the volatility we've seen

25:04.806 --> 25:06.936 align:middle
in repo markets and, you know,

25:06.976 --> 25:08.676 align:middle
potentially some
capital rules as well.

25:09.256 --> 25:11.606 align:middle
Are you all looking
at whether, you know,

25:11.766 --> 25:14.706 align:middle
some tweaks to the liquidity
coverage ratio might help?

25:14.926 --> 25:17.766 align:middle
And, also, what is the status

25:17.766 --> 25:19.656 align:middle
of the net stable
funding ratio rule?

25:19.656 --> 25:21.536 align:middle
Are you all still planning
on putting that out soon?

25:21.886 --> 25:22.316 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:22.546 --> 25:27.136 align:middle
You know, so I think if we
concluded that we needed

25:27.886 --> 25:31.186 align:middle
to raise the level of
required reserves for banks

25:31.186 --> 25:34.216 align:middle
to meet the LCR, we'd
probably raise the level

25:34.216 --> 25:36.606 align:middle
of reserves rather
than lower the LCR.

25:36.606 --> 25:39.696 align:middle
I mean, it's not impossible
that we would come to a view

25:39.696 --> 25:42.516 align:middle
that the LCR is calibrated too
high, but that's not something

25:42.516 --> 25:43.286 align:middle
that we think right now.

25:44.216 --> 25:46.356 align:middle
On the other hand, it might be
that more reserves are needed,

25:46.486 --> 25:48.356 align:middle
in which case we are in a
position to supply them.

25:49.166 --> 25:51.426 align:middle
In terms of the net
stable funding ratio,

25:54.306 --> 25:57.716 align:middle
it's-I believe we put it out
for comment and got comments,

25:58.376 --> 26:01.806 align:middle
and I believe we're
looking at-at finalizing

26:01.806 --> 26:03.046 align:middle
that in the relatively
near future.

26:03.436 --> 26:04.506 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

26:04.676 --> 26:09.266 align:middle
Just-just to follow up, I mean,
in terms of tweaks to the LCR,

26:09.266 --> 26:10.516 align:middle
I mean, there's also
been some talk

26:10.516 --> 26:14.086 align:middle
about potentially giving banks
some room in times of stress

26:14.086 --> 26:16.196 align:middle
to maybe dip into their
liquidity buffers.

26:16.746 --> 26:18.896 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:18.896 --> 26:20.246 align:middle
I think we want banks to,

26:20.556 --> 26:22.586 align:middle
you know-to use their
liquidity buffers in times

26:22.586 --> 26:25.016 align:middle
of stress rather than pull back
from the markets and pull back

26:25.016 --> 26:27.466 align:middle
from serving their
clients as a general rule.

26:29.956 --> 26:30.886 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

26:33.616 --> 26:35.286 align:middle
So, about a month
ago-Edward Lawrence

26:35.286 --> 26:36.336 align:middle
from Fox Business Network.

26:36.336 --> 26:37.496 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

26:37.496 --> 26:39.576 align:middle
About a month ago or so you
said that there's no precedent

26:39.576 --> 26:42.216 align:middle
to integrate trade uncertainty
into monetary policy.

26:42.576 --> 26:45.096 align:middle
In the last few weeks,
have you figured out how

26:45.096 --> 26:47.246 align:middle
to incorporate trade
uncertainty-and this level

26:47.246 --> 26:50.506 align:middle
of trade uncertainty-into
monetary policy going forward?

26:50.506 --> 26:52.656 align:middle
You've talked about
uncertainty many times today.

26:52.826 --> 26:53.946 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:54.296 --> 26:57.916 align:middle
So my point, really,
was that-to start,

26:57.976 --> 27:00.366 align:middle
that trade policy is not
the business of the Fed,

27:00.366 --> 27:02.236 align:middle
it's the business of Congress
and the Administration.

27:02.786 --> 27:05.026 align:middle
But-so why are we
talking about it?

27:05.026 --> 27:06.506 align:middle
We're talking about
it because anything

27:06.506 --> 27:09.256 align:middle
that affects the achievement
of our goals can, in principle,

27:09.256 --> 27:12.146 align:middle
be something that
monetary policy should take

27:12.146 --> 27:12.916 align:middle
into consideration.

27:13.256 --> 27:16.176 align:middle
And our discussions and the
research we have suggests

27:16.176 --> 27:19.046 align:middle
that trade policy is something
that's weighing on the outlook.

27:19.106 --> 27:23.306 align:middle
So I pointed out
in recent remarks

27:23.306 --> 27:25.536 align:middle
that the thing we
can't address, really,

27:25.626 --> 27:31.016 align:middle
is what businesses would like,
which is a settled roadmap

27:31.016 --> 27:32.006 align:middle
for international trade.

27:32.286 --> 27:33.096 align:middle
We can't do that.

27:33.096 --> 27:33.946 align:middle
We don't have that tool.

27:34.296 --> 27:36.916 align:middle
But we do have a
very powerful tool

27:36.916 --> 27:39.136 align:middle
which can counteract
weakness, to some extent,

27:39.136 --> 27:42.136 align:middle
by supporting demand through
sound monetary policy.

27:42.476 --> 27:46.366 align:middle
And we think our policy tools
support economic activity

27:46.836 --> 27:48.656 align:middle
through fairly well-understood
channels:

27:48.826 --> 27:52.546 align:middle
by reducing interest burden and
encouraging consumer purchase

27:52.546 --> 27:55.836 align:middle
of durables, of homes, and
other interest-sensitive items;

27:56.446 --> 27:59.256 align:middle
by creating broadly more
accommodative financial

27:59.256 --> 28:01.636 align:middle
conditions, which support
spending and also investment

28:01.636 --> 28:04.346 align:middle
by businesses; and also
by boosting household

28:04.346 --> 28:05.216 align:middle
and business confidence.

28:05.216 --> 28:08.376 align:middle
So, you know, I don't
want to be heard to say

28:08.376 --> 28:09.676 align:middle
that our tools don't
have an effect.

28:09.676 --> 28:12.726 align:middle
They do. But I was making the
point that there is a piece

28:12.726 --> 28:14.966 align:middle
of this that we really
can't address.

28:15.226 --> 28:15.596 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

28:15.596 --> 28:18.286 align:middle
So have you figured out
how to navigatge it?

28:18.286 --> 28:18.496 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:18.496 --> 28:18.966 align:middle
Well, I think, yeah.

28:19.116 --> 28:20.186 align:middle
I mean, it's a challenge.

28:20.186 --> 28:22.946 align:middle
There's no simple bottom-line
answer where I can say, yes,

28:22.946 --> 28:23.766 align:middle
I've got it for you here.

28:23.766 --> 28:25.586 align:middle
But what it amounts to is this:

28:26.176 --> 28:31.236 align:middle
What you see is probably
the kind

28:31.236 --> 28:34.726 align:middle
of volatility that's
typical of an important,

28:34.986 --> 28:37.916 align:middle
complex, ongoing negotiation.

28:38.316 --> 28:41.126 align:middle
And I think what we need
to do is to try to look

28:41.126 --> 28:45.186 align:middle
through the volatility and
react to the underlying forces,

28:45.186 --> 28:47.156 align:middle
the underlying things
that are happening

28:47.346 --> 28:48.776 align:middle
that are relevant
to our mandate.

28:49.066 --> 28:51.906 align:middle
We don't-we have nothing to
do with setting trade policy

28:51.906 --> 28:53.776 align:middle
or negotiating trade agreements.

28:54.036 --> 28:57.236 align:middle
We're supposed to be reacting on
behalf of the American economy

28:57.756 --> 29:00.256 align:middle
to support maximum
employment and stable prices.

29:00.256 --> 29:02.616 align:middle
So we need to look
through what's a pretty

29:02.616 --> 29:03.656 align:middle
volatile situation.

29:03.656 --> 29:05.636 align:middle
So that means not
overreacting quickly.

29:05.636 --> 29:06.956 align:middle
It means not underreacting, too.

29:06.956 --> 29:09.116 align:middle
So that's really what
we're trying to do.

29:09.726 --> 29:14.686 align:middle
And, you know, I would say, the
outlook is positive in the face

29:14.686 --> 29:16.296 align:middle
of these crosswinds we've felt.

29:16.476 --> 29:20.976 align:middle
And so, to some extent, I
do believe that our shifting

29:21.146 --> 29:23.216 align:middle
to a more accommodative
stance over the course

29:23.216 --> 29:24.416 align:middle
of the year has been one

29:24.416 --> 29:26.416 align:middle
of the reasons why the
outlook has remained favorable.

29:29.896 --> 29:33.646 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

29:35.966 --> 29:39.576 align:middle
Fed funds-Michael McKee from
Bloomberg Radio and Television.

29:39.786 --> 29:43.426 align:middle
Fed funds futures trading since
the statement was released shows

29:43.426 --> 29:45.196 align:middle
that investors still think

29:45.196 --> 29:47.346 align:middle
that another rate cut
is coming this year.

29:47.816 --> 29:50.946 align:middle
On their behalf, let
me ask, what is going

29:50.946 --> 29:56.006 align:middle
to guide Fed policy to
either pull them towards

29:56.116 --> 29:58.606 align:middle
where the dot plot suggests
no more moves this year

29:59.046 --> 30:00.596 align:middle
or keep them in place?

30:00.596 --> 30:02.656 align:middle
Are you reacting to data now?

30:02.656 --> 30:05.216 align:middle
Are you reacting
to your gut feeling

30:05.216 --> 30:07.526 align:middle
about what trade
tweets might mean?

30:07.936 --> 30:11.136 align:middle
Should they just watch
for Jay Powell speeches

30:11.136 --> 30:13.746 align:middle
to decide what's going
to happen going forward?

30:13.986 --> 30:15.876 align:middle
What's the Fed's
reaction function now?

30:16.056 --> 30:17.596 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:17.596 --> 30:23.436 align:middle
Right. So what we are looking
for through all of the data,

30:23.436 --> 30:26.096 align:middle
all the events that are
going on around the world,

30:26.096 --> 30:27.676 align:middle
we'll be looking at, you know,

30:27.676 --> 30:31.256 align:middle
the evolving geopolitical
events, we'll be looking

30:31.256 --> 30:32.506 align:middle
at global growth,
we'll be looking

30:32.536 --> 30:34.176 align:middle
at trade policy uncertainty.

30:34.346 --> 30:35.416 align:middle
Most importantly,
we'll be looking

30:35.416 --> 30:36.816 align:middle
at the performance
of the U.S. economy.

30:37.266 --> 30:41.726 align:middle
We'll be looking for things
that are affecting the outlook

30:42.456 --> 30:44.446 align:middle
for the U.S. economy,
particularly the outlook

30:44.446 --> 30:46.696 align:middle
as it relates to maximum
employment and stable prices.

30:46.696 --> 30:47.686 align:middle
So all of those things

30:47.686 --> 30:51.406 align:middle
in principle can affect the
achievement of our goals.

30:52.326 --> 30:55.646 align:middle
It's an unusual situation,
because, you know,

30:55.776 --> 30:59.196 align:middle
the U.S. economy itself-the
largest part of it,

30:59.196 --> 31:02.116 align:middle
the consumer part of
it-is in strong shape.

31:02.686 --> 31:04.416 align:middle
The manufacturing part, less so.

31:04.496 --> 31:07.836 align:middle
But, overall, you see an
economy that-I think, generally,

31:07.916 --> 31:11.406 align:middle
forecasts show growth similar
to our own, forecasts coming

31:11.406 --> 31:14.456 align:middle
in about 2 percent, which
is a good, solid year.

31:15.166 --> 31:18.706 align:middle
So the difference here is,
we have significant, really,

31:20.066 --> 31:23.736 align:middle
risks to that outlook from not
just the geopolitical events,

31:23.736 --> 31:25.486 align:middle
but also from slowing
global growth

31:25.486 --> 31:26.496 align:middle
and trade policy uncertainty.

31:26.496 --> 31:29.246 align:middle
So we'll be looking
at all of that

31:29.246 --> 31:31.626 align:middle
and also financial
market conditions

31:31.686 --> 31:33.436 align:middle
and how they are
affecting the outlook.

31:33.886 --> 31:38.726 align:middle
I can't-it's a challenging time,
I admit it, but we really have

31:38.726 --> 31:39.886 align:middle
to be open to all those things.

31:40.416 --> 31:41.896 align:middle
We're not on a preset course.

31:42.246 --> 31:44.336 align:middle
We're going to be making
decisions meeting by meeting

31:44.436 --> 31:47.976 align:middle
as we see this, and, you
know, we'll try to be

31:47.976 --> 31:49.376 align:middle
as transparent as
we can as we go.

31:49.876 --> 31:52.926 align:middle
DONNA BORAK.

31:55.116 --> 31:55.656 align:middle
Donna Borak.

31:55.656 --> 31:56.726 align:middle
Hi, Chairman Powell.

31:56.726 --> 31:58.456 align:middle
Donna Borak with CNN.

31:58.456 --> 31:59.606 align:middle
With the rate cut today

31:59.606 --> 32:02.556 align:middle
and potentially another modest
adjustment coming down the road,

32:02.916 --> 32:06.226 align:middle
do you worry about lessening the
Fed's firepower should there be

32:06.226 --> 32:06.896 align:middle
a recession?

32:06.986 --> 32:08.626 align:middle
And is there any scenario

32:08.626 --> 32:11.236 align:middle
in which you would envision
rates drifting lower

32:11.236 --> 32:12.606 align:middle
into negative territory?

32:13.236 --> 32:14.556 align:middle
And are there any other tools

32:14.556 --> 32:16.756 align:middle
that you could use
before having to go there?

32:17.316 --> 32:20.306 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

32:20.556 --> 32:28.586 align:middle
You know, in terms of firepower,
I think the general principle,

32:28.586 --> 32:32.326 align:middle
as I mentioned earlier, is, it
can be a mistake to try to hold

32:32.326 --> 32:35.906 align:middle
onto your firepower until
a downturn gains momentum,

32:35.906 --> 32:39.306 align:middle
and then-so there's a
fair amount of research

32:39.796 --> 32:41.306 align:middle
that would show that
that's the case.

32:41.376 --> 32:44.376 align:middle
Now, I think that principle
needs to be applied carefully

32:44.376 --> 32:45.696 align:middle
to the situation at hand.

32:46.316 --> 32:48.856 align:middle
What we believe we're facing
here-what we think we're facing

32:48.856 --> 32:51.256 align:middle
here is a situation
which can be addressed

32:51.256 --> 32:53.966 align:middle
and should be addressed
with moderate adjustments

32:53.966 --> 32:55.296 align:middle
to the federal funds rate.

32:55.566 --> 32:59.886 align:middle
As I mentioned, we
are watching carefully

32:59.886 --> 33:01.166 align:middle
to see whether that is the case.

33:01.336 --> 33:04.616 align:middle
If, in fact, the economy weakens
more, then we're prepared

33:04.616 --> 33:08.116 align:middle
to be aggressive, and
we'll do so if it turns

33:09.196 --> 33:10.406 align:middle
out to be appropriate.

33:10.896 --> 33:12.296 align:middle
You mentioned negative
interest rates.

33:12.296 --> 33:16.286 align:middle
So negative interest rates
is something that we looked

33:16.286 --> 33:19.406 align:middle
at during the financial
crisis and chose not to do.

33:19.406 --> 33:22.006 align:middle
We chose to-after we got to
the effective lower bound,

33:22.006 --> 33:25.206 align:middle
we chose to do a lot of
aggressive forward guidance

33:25.636 --> 33:28.116 align:middle
and also large-scale
asset purchases,

33:28.116 --> 33:30.936 align:middle
and those were the two
unconventional monetary policy

33:30.936 --> 33:32.396 align:middle
tools that we used extensively.

33:32.456 --> 33:34.676 align:middle
We feel that they
worked fairly well.

33:35.256 --> 33:37.086 align:middle
We did not use negative rates.

33:37.686 --> 33:40.506 align:middle
And I think if we
were to find ourselves

33:40.506 --> 33:43.936 align:middle
at some future date again at
the effective lower bound-again,

33:43.936 --> 33:47.086 align:middle
not something we are
expecting-then I think we would

33:47.756 --> 33:53.436 align:middle
look at using large-scale asset
purchases and forward guidance.

33:53.746 --> 33:56.316 align:middle
I do not think we'd be looking
at using negative rates.

33:56.556 --> 33:58.666 align:middle
I just don't think those will
be at the top of our list.

33:58.986 --> 34:01.776 align:middle
By the way, we are in the middle
of a monetary policy review

34:01.776 --> 34:04.296 align:middle
where we're looking through
all of these questions

34:04.296 --> 34:07.836 align:middle
about the longer-run
framework-the strategy, tools,

34:07.836 --> 34:10.946 align:middle
and communications-and we expect
that to be completed sometime

34:11.086 --> 34:14.976 align:middle
around the middle of next year.

34:16.166 --> 34:19.526 align:middle
DON LEE. Don Lee
with the L.A. Times.

34:19.526 --> 34:24.766 align:middle
How much-can you talk
about the mechanism

34:24.766 --> 34:29.476 align:middle
in which the two rate cuts
will affect the real economy?

34:29.476 --> 34:34.206 align:middle
And how much-to what extent will
it offset the negative effects

34:34.206 --> 34:36.936 align:middle
of the trade uncertainty
and tensions?

34:37.826 --> 34:38.866 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:39.076 --> 34:42.746 align:middle
So, in terms of how our rate
cuts will affect the real

34:42.786 --> 34:46.116 align:middle
economy, first, we think
monetary policy works with,

34:46.946 --> 34:49.066 align:middle
as Friedman said, long
and variable lags.

34:49.066 --> 34:53.076 align:middle
So I think the real effects
will be felt over time.

34:53.586 --> 34:56.446 align:middle
But, you know, we think

34:56.446 --> 35:00.876 align:middle
that lower interest rates
will reduce interest burden

35:00.876 --> 35:03.876 align:middle
for borrowers, so that
interest-sensitive things

35:03.876 --> 35:06.396 align:middle
like housing and durable
goods and other things

35:06.746 --> 35:09.636 align:middle
like that-cars-it supports
purchases of those.

35:10.206 --> 35:11.576 align:middle
Just, again, broadly,

35:11.576 --> 35:14.546 align:middle
more accommodative financial
conditions-higher asset prices.

35:14.606 --> 35:16.886 align:middle
That's-the models
and the data show

35:16.886 --> 35:18.686 align:middle
that that's another
powerful channel.

35:19.146 --> 35:20.736 align:middle
I also think there's
a confidence channel.

35:20.736 --> 35:26.526 align:middle
You see-you see household
and business confidence turn

35:26.526 --> 35:29.476 align:middle
up when financial conditions
become more accommodative.

35:29.476 --> 35:32.956 align:middle
So I think, through all of those
channels, monetary policy works.

35:33.326 --> 35:35.656 align:middle
It isn't, you know,
precisely the right tool

35:35.656 --> 35:39.296 align:middle
for every single possible
negative thing that can happen

35:39.296 --> 35:42.016 align:middle
to the economy, but,
nonetheless, it broadly works,

35:42.536 --> 35:44.376 align:middle
and, you know, we're going
to use the tool we have.

35:44.526 --> 35:46.846 align:middle
And if it comes to it,
we'll use all of our tools.

35:47.506 --> 35:50.726 align:middle
So that's how we think it works
and how we think it's working.

35:50.726 --> 35:54.776 align:middle
DON LEE. And how much will
it offset the trade impact,

35:55.306 --> 35:55.956 align:middle
do you think?

35:56.036 --> 35:56.446 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:56.446 --> 35:57.556 align:middle
It's very hard to say.

35:57.556 --> 35:59.536 align:middle
It's-you know, it's
tough to say.

35:59.536 --> 36:02.096 align:middle
We'll-we'll use our tools

36:02.096 --> 36:05.766 align:middle
to offset negative-that's really
the job of monetary policy, is,

36:05.766 --> 36:08.556 align:middle
to the extent it can,
to offset, you know,

36:08.736 --> 36:10.416 align:middle
things that drive us away

36:10.416 --> 36:12.506 align:middle
from maximum employment
and stable prices.

36:14.236 --> 36:15.736 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

36:16.146 --> 36:19.506 align:middle
I'm Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

36:20.156 --> 36:22.196 align:middle
Chair Powell, are you worried

36:22.366 --> 36:24.996 align:middle
that the low interest
rates are adding to,

36:24.996 --> 36:28.396 align:middle
or could create a bubble of,
consumer and corporate debt

36:28.836 --> 36:31.106 align:middle
that could make it more
difficult for, especially,

36:31.106 --> 36:34.946 align:middle
consumers to recover
from the next recession

36:34.946 --> 36:36.366 align:middle
or survive the next recession?

36:36.456 --> 36:37.016 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:37.236 --> 36:39.696 align:middle
You know, so, if you look at-if
you actually look at households,

36:39.786 --> 36:41.696 align:middle
households are in
very strong shape.

36:41.746 --> 36:42.926 align:middle
They're less levered.

36:43.026 --> 36:44.026 align:middle
They've got less debt.

36:44.526 --> 36:47.716 align:middle
They've got more income relative
to their interest requirements.

36:48.336 --> 36:51.706 align:middle
And they're in very good
shape-much better shape

36:51.706 --> 36:53.806 align:middle
than they were in before
the financial crisis.

36:53.806 --> 36:56.696 align:middle
So the household
sector as a sort

36:56.746 --> 37:00.046 align:middle
of aggregate matter
is in very good shape.

37:00.046 --> 37:01.726 align:middle
That doesn't mean that
every single person

37:01.726 --> 37:04.316 align:middle
in the household sector is
in good shape, but, overall,

37:04.576 --> 37:05.616 align:middle
it's really not a concern.

37:06.546 --> 37:10.056 align:middle
The business sector is something
that, you know, we've talked

37:10.056 --> 37:15.206 align:middle
about a lot and studied a lot,
and the situation there is

37:15.206 --> 37:19.716 align:middle
that the level of
debt relative to GDP

37:19.716 --> 37:23.046 align:middle
in the business sector
is at a high level.

37:23.046 --> 37:24.876 align:middle
However, so is the size
of the business sector.

37:24.876 --> 37:29.726 align:middle
So, actually, the business
sector itself is not materially

37:29.726 --> 37:30.876 align:middle
higher leveraged than it was.

37:30.876 --> 37:33.746 align:middle
Nonetheless, there are a lot
of highly levered companies,

37:33.746 --> 37:36.926 align:middle
and that's the kind of thing
that happens during a long cycle

37:36.926 --> 37:40.516 align:middle
when there aren't downturns
for, now, into our 11th year.

37:40.516 --> 37:43.446 align:middle
You get-you know,
you do get that kind

37:43.446 --> 37:44.886 align:middle
of phenomenon in a long cycle.

37:44.886 --> 37:46.386 align:middle
So that's something
we're monitoring,

37:46.386 --> 37:51.266 align:middle
and I think our view still
is that that's a real issue,

37:51.266 --> 37:55.506 align:middle
but what it really represents
is a potential amplifier

37:55.726 --> 37:57.316 align:middle
of a macroeconomic downturn.

37:57.636 --> 38:01.256 align:middle
It does not have the
makings of anything

38:01.256 --> 38:02.646 align:middle
that would undermine
the workings

38:02.646 --> 38:04.266 align:middle
of the financial
system, for example,

38:04.296 --> 38:07.716 align:middle
or itself create a shock that
would turn the economy down.

38:07.946 --> 38:09.756 align:middle
It's more of-it's
more of an amplifier.

38:10.146 --> 38:13.196 align:middle
We take it very seriously,
though, and, you know,

38:13.196 --> 38:14.766 align:middle
we're monitoring it carefully.

38:14.766 --> 38:20.216 align:middle
We're actually looking-the
Financial Stability Board is

38:20.216 --> 38:23.816 align:middle
actually conducting a
project right now to identify

38:23.816 --> 38:25.596 align:middle
where these loans are
held all around the world.

38:25.596 --> 38:28.156 align:middle
So it's the subject of a lot of
study and work, and, you know,

38:28.156 --> 38:29.826 align:middle
we're trying to keep
on top of it.

38:33.216 --> 38:34.106 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

38:34.606 --> 38:36.116 align:middle
Thank you, Chairman Powell.

38:36.116 --> 38:37.456 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

38:38.126 --> 38:40.246 align:middle
I'm kind of hearing
two things from you.

38:40.466 --> 38:42.256 align:middle
You're saying that the
economy is doing well,

38:42.826 --> 38:45.256 align:middle
but there's this
sense with people

38:45.256 --> 38:47.776 align:middle
that the economy is actually
starting to slow now.

38:48.326 --> 38:51.306 align:middle
And people are-more and
more, you talk-there's talk

38:51.306 --> 38:55.446 align:middle
about recession, so you-and
even the Fed thinks the downside

38:55.976 --> 38:57.106 align:middle
risks are rising.

38:57.946 --> 39:01.526 align:middle
So is the economy over the next
year-between now and the end

39:01.526 --> 39:04.846 align:middle
of next year, do you think GDP
growth is going to hold steady,

39:04.846 --> 39:07.266 align:middle
and the unemployment
rate-could you talk about, just,

39:07.746 --> 39:10.006 align:middle
how do you see the economy
evolving over the next year?

39:10.276 --> 39:10.516 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:10.516 --> 39:13.386 align:middle
You know, so, I think-and my
colleagues and I, I think,

39:13.386 --> 39:16.166 align:middle
all think that the
most likely case is

39:16.166 --> 39:19.526 align:middle
for continued moderate growth,
continued strong labor market,

39:19.766 --> 39:21.406 align:middle
and inflation moving
back up to 2 percent.

39:21.666 --> 39:25.806 align:middle
And I think that's, by the way,
widely shared among forecasters.

39:26.776 --> 39:29.686 align:middle
You know, the issue is
more the risks to that.

39:29.736 --> 39:32.056 align:middle
You have downside risks here,
and we've talked about them.

39:32.056 --> 39:35.366 align:middle
It's that global growth will
have an effect on U.S. growth

39:35.366 --> 39:38.366 align:middle
over time- less so than for many
other economies, but, still,

39:38.816 --> 39:41.396 align:middle
there's a sector of our
economy that's exposed to that.

39:41.956 --> 39:46.876 align:middle
Trade policy uncertainty also
has-apparently has an effect.

39:46.876 --> 39:49.296 align:middle
So-and you can see some weakness

39:49.296 --> 39:50.946 align:middle
in the U.S. economy
because of all that.

39:51.046 --> 39:53.716 align:middle
But, nonetheless-so the
job of monetary policy is

39:53.716 --> 39:57.986 align:middle
to adjust both to ensure
against those downside risks,

39:58.206 --> 40:01.036 align:middle
but also to support
the economy in light

40:01.036 --> 40:02.816 align:middle
of the existing weakness
that we do see.

40:03.646 --> 40:05.166 align:middle
So we're not-as I mentioned,

40:05.166 --> 40:07.326 align:middle
we're not-we don't
see a recession.

40:07.326 --> 40:08.786 align:middle
We're not forecasting
a recession.

40:09.326 --> 40:11.096 align:middle
But we are adjusting
monetary policy

40:11.096 --> 40:13.466 align:middle
in a more accommodative
direction to try

40:13.466 --> 40:15.946 align:middle
to support what is, in
fact, a favorable outlook.

40:17.126 --> 40:17.916 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

40:19.976 --> 40:21.916 align:middle
And the inverted yield curve

40:21.966 --> 40:26.346 align:middle
that we hear is the bond market
signaling recession-what do

40:26.346 --> 40:31.376 align:middle
you-is that-so that's not-that
signal is not pertinent to you?

40:31.376 --> 40:32.316 align:middle
Or where do you- CHAIR POWELL.

40:32.316 --> 40:35.506 align:middle
No, we do-so we monitor
the yield curve carefully,

40:35.506 --> 40:40.426 align:middle
along with a large, wide
range of financial conditions.

40:40.526 --> 40:46.066 align:middle
We don't-so, it's
not-there's no one thing

40:46.066 --> 40:48.766 align:middle
that is dispositive among
all financial conditions.

40:48.766 --> 40:52.166 align:middle
The yield curve is something
that we follow carefully.

40:52.846 --> 40:56.156 align:middle
And, again, based on our
assessment of all the data,

40:56.156 --> 40:57.916 align:middle
we still think it's
a positive outlook.

40:58.486 --> 41:01.776 align:middle
The thing-so, just to talk
about the current situation,

41:02.136 --> 41:07.126 align:middle
you see-you saw long-term
rates move down a whole lot

41:07.196 --> 41:09.576 align:middle
and then retrace
two-thirds of that move

41:09.576 --> 41:10.686 align:middle
in the space of a few days.

41:10.686 --> 41:12.176 align:middle
So I think what really matters

41:12.176 --> 41:14.286 align:middle
for all financial
conditions generally is

41:14.286 --> 41:16.616 align:middle
when there are changes-material
changes

41:16.616 --> 41:18.396 align:middle
that are sustained
for a period of time.

41:19.186 --> 41:23.266 align:middle
So-but why are long-term
rates low?

41:23.266 --> 41:25.826 align:middle
There are a number-there can
be a signal about expectations

41:25.826 --> 41:27.246 align:middle
about growth there for sure,

41:27.246 --> 41:29.416 align:middle
but there can also just
be low term premiums.

41:29.936 --> 41:32.066 align:middle
For example, just-well,
it can just be

41:32.066 --> 41:36.326 align:middle
that there's this large
quantity of negative-yielding

41:36.326 --> 41:38.726 align:middle
and very low-yielding sovereign
debt around the world, and,

41:38.726 --> 41:41.266 align:middle
inevitably, that's
exerting downward pressure

41:41.336 --> 41:44.936 align:middle
on U.S. sovereign rates without
really necessarily having an

41:44.936 --> 41:45.756 align:middle
independent signal.

41:45.966 --> 41:48.836 align:middle
Nonetheless, that is a signal
about weak global growth,

41:48.836 --> 41:51.706 align:middle
probably, and weak global
growth would affect us.

41:52.296 --> 41:53.656 align:middle
So global capital markets

41:53.656 --> 41:55.646 align:middle
and the global economy
are quite integrated,

41:55.646 --> 41:57.796 align:middle
so this is something we
pay careful-we're not going

41:57.796 --> 41:59.386 align:middle
to be dismissive
about the yield curve,

41:59.456 --> 42:03.306 align:middle
but-and I think you can tell, on
the Committee, there's a range

42:03.306 --> 42:05.426 align:middle
of views-there are some
who are very focused

42:05.426 --> 42:07.136 align:middle
on the yield curve,
others not so much.

42:07.696 --> 42:11.546 align:middle
You know, from my perspective,
you watch it carefully, and,

42:12.176 --> 42:14.306 align:middle
you know, I think you need
to be asking yourself a lot

42:14.306 --> 42:17.416 align:middle
of questions if the yield curve
is inverted as to why that is

42:18.106 --> 42:19.996 align:middle
and how long it's sustained.

42:22.236 --> 42:25.286 align:middle
PAUL KIERNAN.

42:25.756 --> 42:26.516 align:middle
Hi, Chairman Powell.

42:26.516 --> 42:29.036 align:middle
Paul Kiernan from
Dow Jones Newswires.

42:30.706 --> 42:36.176 align:middle
You mentioned trade policy being
a complex, ongoing discussion.

42:37.536 --> 42:44.336 align:middle
What is your rule for stopping
as far as interest rate cuts go?

42:44.336 --> 42:48.176 align:middle
You referred to this as
a "midcycle adjustment."

42:48.656 --> 42:53.576 align:middle
The median dot suggests no
more rate cuts, but, you know,

42:53.576 --> 42:56.416 align:middle
if we get continued kind of
back-and-forth between the U.S.

42:56.416 --> 42:59.666 align:middle
and China, trade policy
uncertainty is going

42:59.666 --> 43:00.496 align:middle
to remain heightened.

43:01.016 --> 43:04.536 align:middle
So, you know, under what
circumstances would you say,

43:04.536 --> 43:08.256 align:middle
"You know what, I think we've
cut enough, we stop now"?

43:08.256 --> 43:12.276 align:middle
And, secondly, as leader
of this institution,

43:13.006 --> 43:16.976 align:middle
have you felt a need to take
any steps to boost, like,

43:16.976 --> 43:18.886 align:middle
employee morale at a time

43:19.216 --> 43:22.286 align:middle
when the President is
constantly criticizing the Fed?

43:22.406 --> 43:23.996 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

43:24.536 --> 43:26.776 align:middle
You know, I'd love to be
able to articulate a simple,

43:26.776 --> 43:29.686 align:middle
straightforward stopping rule,
but it's really just going to be

43:30.246 --> 43:32.216 align:middle
when we think we've done enough.

43:32.216 --> 43:33.496 align:middle
And, you know, our
eyes are open.

43:33.496 --> 43:34.696 align:middle
We're watching the situation.

43:34.696 --> 43:35.786 align:middle
We've cut rates twice.

43:35.946 --> 43:38.506 align:middle
We've moved, really, through
the course of this year,

43:38.506 --> 43:42.746 align:middle
as I discussed, and, you
know, we see ourselves

43:42.806 --> 43:45.246 align:middle
as taking actions to
sustain the expansion

43:45.246 --> 43:46.726 align:middle
and thereby achieve our goals.

43:47.646 --> 43:51.276 align:middle
And if you look at sort of
the things that are happening

43:51.276 --> 43:54.246 align:middle
in the economy, I think I
personally see a high value

43:54.826 --> 43:56.016 align:middle
in sustaining the expansion,

43:56.016 --> 44:00.546 align:middle
because we really are
reaching-this positive economy

44:00.546 --> 44:01.766 align:middle
is reaching communities

44:01.766 --> 44:03.196 align:middle
that haven't been
reached in a long time.

44:03.916 --> 44:06.426 align:middle
There would be great benefit
in having that last as long

44:06.426 --> 44:07.316 align:middle
as possible, that's all.

44:07.876 --> 44:10.516 align:middle
So I don't have a specific
stopping rule for you,

44:10.516 --> 44:12.286 align:middle
but I think we're
watching carefully,

44:12.286 --> 44:13.716 align:middle
and there will come
a time, I suspect,

44:13.716 --> 44:16.626 align:middle
when we think we've done enough,
but there may also come a time

44:16.626 --> 44:19.386 align:middle
when the economy worsens,
and we would then have

44:19.386 --> 44:20.466 align:middle
to cut more aggressively.

44:20.466 --> 44:21.036 align:middle
We don't know.

44:21.036 --> 44:22.546 align:middle
We're going to be
watching things carefully.

44:23.216 --> 44:27.696 align:middle
The incoming data and the
evolving situation and-that's

44:27.696 --> 44:30.996 align:middle
what's going to guide
our-guide us on that path.

44:31.276 --> 44:32.756 align:middle
In terms of the morale
of the institution,

44:32.756 --> 44:33.786 align:middle
I would say it's very high.

44:34.176 --> 44:35.056 align:middle
We're very unified.

44:35.056 --> 44:39.546 align:middle
We feel like we're doing
the best job we can

44:39.546 --> 44:40.546 align:middle
in serving the American people.

44:43.636 --> 44:45.416 align:middle
HANNAH LANG.

44:46.686 --> 44:47.876 align:middle
Hi, Chairman.

44:48.056 --> 44:49.466 align:middle
Hannah Lang with
American Banker.

44:49.696 --> 44:53.496 align:middle
It's been reported that the CFPB
is investigating Bank of America

44:53.496 --> 44:55.776 align:middle
for opening unauthorized
accounts.

44:55.846 --> 44:58.856 align:middle
I'm wondering if the Fed is
also investigating this and,

44:58.856 --> 45:01.806 align:middle
given the pending order against
Wells Fargo, if you're concerned

45:02.206 --> 45:03.916 align:middle
that these banks are
too big to manage.

45:03.916 --> 45:04.896 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

45:05.266 --> 45:08.326 align:middle
You know, so I saw the headline,
like, this morning during all

45:08.326 --> 45:09.366 align:middle
of the preparation
and everything

45:09.896 --> 45:10.816 align:middle
and this morning's meeting.

45:10.816 --> 45:12.626 align:middle
I really don't have
anything for you on that.

45:13.196 --> 45:16.536 align:middle
I will say about Wells
Fargo that, you know,

45:16.536 --> 45:19.306 align:middle
there were quite wide
breakdowns in risk management

45:19.306 --> 45:22.676 align:middle
which resulted in, you know,
mistreatment of consumers

45:22.676 --> 45:26.406 align:middle
that we know was quite
harmful to the consumers

45:26.406 --> 45:28.126 align:middle
and to the image
of the institution.

45:28.176 --> 45:30.586 align:middle
I have no idea whether
that's what happened

45:30.586 --> 45:31.256 align:middle
at Bank of America.

45:31.256 --> 45:36.026 align:middle
I really don't know,
standing here today.

45:37.966 --> 45:43.606 align:middle
STEVEN BECKNER.

45:45.146 --> 45:48.136 align:middle
Steve Beckner filing
for NPR, freelance.

45:48.936 --> 45:52.596 align:middle
The Fed and your fellow
central banks have been sort

45:53.126 --> 45:56.386 align:middle
of exploring the far
reaches of what's possible

45:56.986 --> 46:02.566 align:middle
for monetary policy, even going
so far as to make rates negative

46:02.566 --> 46:05.196 align:middle
in some cases, with
mixed results.

46:05.196 --> 46:08.016 align:middle
Meanwhile, fiscal
and regulatory,

46:08.016 --> 46:10.796 align:middle
not to mention trade, policy
are pursuing their own

46:11.476 --> 46:12.876 align:middle
separate courses.

46:12.876 --> 46:18.296 align:middle
As you and your colleagues do
this monetary policy framework

46:18.296 --> 46:24.496 align:middle
review, do you ever consider the
limitations of monetary policy?

46:24.496 --> 46:29.846 align:middle
Should you be more explicit
about what monetary policy can

46:30.356 --> 46:33.386 align:middle
and cannot do in
this environment?

46:34.276 --> 46:34.886 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:34.886 --> 46:38.706 align:middle
You know, we try to be clear
about that, but, really,

46:38.706 --> 46:44.236 align:middle
I think our job is to use
our tools as best we can

46:44.236 --> 46:46.766 align:middle
to achieve-you know,
to do the jobs

46:46.766 --> 46:48.266 align:middle
that Congress has assigned us,

46:48.266 --> 46:51.366 align:middle
which is achieve maximum
employment and stable prices.

46:51.896 --> 46:52.686 align:middle
That's our real job.

46:53.586 --> 46:56.336 align:middle
In terms of giving the fiscal
authorities-who, by the way,

46:57.006 --> 47:00.036 align:middle
are the ones who created
us-advice about how

47:00.036 --> 47:01.496 align:middle
to do their job, you know,

47:01.496 --> 47:03.946 align:middle
we keep that at a
high level, I think.

47:04.106 --> 47:08.076 align:middle
And, at a high level, yes, I
would say, and I've said before,

47:08.196 --> 47:13.876 align:middle
that it's really fiscal
policy that is more powerful

47:13.876 --> 47:15.176 align:middle
and that has much more

47:15.176 --> 47:19.346 align:middle
to do with-fiscal policy
can do those things

47:19.346 --> 47:22.496 align:middle
that will increase the
longer-run growth rate

47:22.496 --> 47:24.926 align:middle
of the United States by
improving productivity

47:24.976 --> 47:28.266 align:middle
and labor force participation
and the skills

47:28.266 --> 47:29.326 align:middle
and aptitudes of workers.

47:29.326 --> 47:31.386 align:middle
All of that comes from
the private sector,

47:31.386 --> 47:33.776 align:middle
but also from more of
the kinds of things

47:33.776 --> 47:35.666 align:middle
that can be done
with fiscal policy.

47:36.346 --> 47:41.406 align:middle
Over the long run, we can't
really affect the growth rate

47:41.406 --> 47:42.276 align:middle
of the United States.

47:42.276 --> 47:43.206 align:middle
The potential growth rate

47:43.206 --> 47:45.606 align:middle
of the United States is not a
function of monetary policy.

47:45.606 --> 47:47.676 align:middle
It's a function of other things.

47:47.926 --> 47:55.406 align:middle
So I-I try to be clear about
that, and so-but, ultimately,

47:55.886 --> 47:58.646 align:middle
fiscal-fiscal authorities
will do what they

47:58.646 --> 47:59.976 align:middle
seem-deem appropriate.

48:01.206 --> 48:02.726 align:middle
SAMIRA HUSSAIN.

48:05.056 --> 48:08.156 align:middle
Samira Hussain, BBC News.

48:08.156 --> 48:11.606 align:middle
Mr. Trump has been a
very vocal critic of you

48:11.606 --> 48:14.146 align:middle
and your colleagues, recently
calling you "boneheads"

48:14.146 --> 48:17.946 align:middle
and just now has called you
a "terrible communicator."

48:18.376 --> 48:20.436 align:middle
How do you respond
to these criticisms?

48:20.526 --> 48:23.156 align:middle
And any regrets to have
this many press conferences?

48:23.476 --> 48:23.746 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

48:24.356 --> 48:28.816 align:middle
I don't. I'm not going to
change my practice here today

48:28.866 --> 48:31.916 align:middle
of not responding to comments

48:31.916 --> 48:33.956 align:middle
or addressing comments
made by elected officials.

48:34.306 --> 48:39.036 align:middle
I will just say that I continue
to believe that the independence

48:39.036 --> 48:39.846 align:middle
of the Federal Reserve

48:40.416 --> 48:43.966 align:middle
from direct political control
has served the public well

48:43.966 --> 48:44.586 align:middle
over time.

48:45.356 --> 48:47.896 align:middle
And I assure you that my
colleagues and I will continue

48:47.896 --> 48:50.076 align:middle
to conduct monetary
policy without regard

48:50.076 --> 48:51.326 align:middle
to political considerations.

48:51.326 --> 48:54.556 align:middle
We're going to use our best
judgment based on facts,

48:54.756 --> 48:58.066 align:middle
evidence, and objective
analysis in pursuing our goals,

48:58.916 --> 49:02.056 align:middle
and that's what I
have to say on it.

49:02.766 --> 49:04.146 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

49:04.416 --> 49:09.026 align:middle
Hi, Chairman Powell.

49:09.026 --> 49:10.176 align:middle
Brian Cheung with Yahoo Finance.

49:10.176 --> 49:11.596 align:middle
Thanks for taking my question.

49:11.596 --> 49:14.426 align:middle
As we saw with falling yields,
at least up until the beginning

49:14.426 --> 49:16.376 align:middle
of this month, there's
been a lot of demand

49:16.376 --> 49:19.106 align:middle
for U.S. Treasuries, and even
that was partly maybe to blame

49:19.106 --> 49:22.186 align:middle
for the liquidity crunch in repo
markets that we saw this week.

49:22.186 --> 49:24.996 align:middle
I mean, does the Fed have
concerns over the impacts

49:24.996 --> 49:26.686 align:middle
of a global glut for U.S. debt?

49:26.686 --> 49:28.246 align:middle
Is that a conversation
that you also have

49:28.706 --> 49:31.536 align:middle
with Treasury Secretary
Mnuchin-about what the proper

49:31.536 --> 49:34.396 align:middle
way to maybe address some of
the challenges down the road

49:34.396 --> 49:37.476 align:middle
with that type of, kind of,
heavy interest globally?

49:37.896 --> 49:38.106 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

49:38.616 --> 49:39.346 align:middle
Not really, no.

49:39.346 --> 49:41.126 align:middle
That's really-that's
really Treasury's job

49:41.126 --> 49:44.086 align:middle
and Congress's job, in terms of,
well, there's how much to spend,

49:44.086 --> 49:45.426 align:middle
and how big the deficits are,

49:45.426 --> 49:46.966 align:middle
and then there's
how to finance it.

49:47.326 --> 49:49.036 align:middle
And, you know, none
of that really calls

49:49.036 --> 49:50.196 align:middle
for advice from the Fed.

49:50.366 --> 49:53.116 align:middle
We take-we take fiscal
policy pretty much

49:53.116 --> 49:54.906 align:middle
as exogenous to our work.

49:54.906 --> 49:58.756 align:middle
Now, that doesn't stop
us, from time to time,

49:58.836 --> 50:01.516 align:middle
from saying that we
think it's important

50:01.566 --> 50:05.886 align:middle
that the U.S. fiscal picture
return to a sustainable footing,

50:06.566 --> 50:07.996 align:middle
and, you know, right
now it's not.

50:08.026 --> 50:09.716 align:middle
That's been the case
for a long time,

50:09.926 --> 50:13.656 align:middle
and that's something we will
have to address, and a good time

50:13.656 --> 50:15.266 align:middle
to do it is when the
economy is strong.

50:15.266 --> 50:19.726 align:middle
So we limit ourselves to
high-level statements like that.

50:20.496 --> 50:21.486 align:middle
HEATHER LONG.

50:23.056 --> 50:27.066 align:middle
Hi, Heather Long from
the Washington Post.

50:27.066 --> 50:30.896 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, in your
view, is there any risk

50:30.896 --> 50:34.096 align:middle
to the United States having
much higher interest rates

50:34.096 --> 50:36.906 align:middle
than Europe and Japan and
other parts of the world?

50:36.906 --> 50:39.726 align:middle
Is there any risk to the U.S.
economy to that divergence

50:39.726 --> 50:41.506 align:middle
or any risk to the
global economy?

50:42.086 --> 50:42.446 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

50:42.446 --> 50:44.726 align:middle
Yes, so I guess I
would say it this way.

50:44.726 --> 50:50.786 align:middle
Global capital markets are
highly integrated, and,

50:51.626 --> 50:54.776 align:middle
you know, our rates-our long
rates are definitely being

50:54.776 --> 50:58.986 align:middle
pulled down by the very, very
low rates that are abroad.

50:59.306 --> 51:01.906 align:middle
And, you know, the way I
would characterize it is this:

51:01.906 --> 51:06.496 align:middle
that low rates abroad are
a symbol-or a sign, rather,

51:07.016 --> 51:11.126 align:middle
of weak global growth,
expectations of low inflation,

51:11.286 --> 51:16.456 align:middle
low growth, and, you know, just
kind of a lack of policy space

51:16.456 --> 51:20.096 align:middle
to move against or
ideas about how to break

51:20.096 --> 51:21.156 align:middle
out of that low equilibrium.

51:21.266 --> 51:23.036 align:middle
Now, that has implications
for us.

51:23.456 --> 51:26.336 align:middle
You know, we-in a
world where economies

51:26.336 --> 51:28.486 align:middle
and financial markets
are tightly integrated,

51:28.676 --> 51:30.156 align:middle
that matters for
the U.S. economy.

51:30.496 --> 51:32.956 align:middle
So that's going to
pull down U.S. rates,

51:32.956 --> 51:37.146 align:middle
and U.S. financial conditions
can tighten because of that.

51:37.276 --> 51:39.556 align:middle
And so I think we put
all-all of that goes

51:39.556 --> 51:42.656 align:middle
into our thinking
and into our models.

51:42.656 --> 51:44.286 align:middle
We do understand
how the-you know,

51:44.286 --> 51:46.656 align:middle
how the international
sector interfaces

51:46.656 --> 51:47.696 align:middle
with the U.S. economy.

51:47.696 --> 51:49.226 align:middle
We take that into account

51:49.226 --> 51:50.916 align:middle
in setting our interest
rate policy.

51:52.266 --> 51:57.706 align:middle
Thank you very much.

